\par
Picture \par
\par
A cluster of anxious courtiers circle around their duke, a man of notorious inscrutability and dangerous volatility. One of them rashly responds to his master\rquote s expression of a wish to participate in military action by advising him that princes \lquote should not desire to go to war in person\rquote . When challenged, the courtier makes matters worse by asserting that such employment is beneath the dignity of a prince, and that, therefore, the latter would be much \lquote better [to] do it by deputy\rquote . This provokes a stinging rebuke, since, in the Duke\rquote s view, such unworthy idleness would deprive the nobleman guilty of it of the opportunity to acquire honour \f1\emdash  that indispensable aristocratic attribute. \par
\par
Unforeseen consequences \par
\par
This tense encounter occurs early in John Webster\rquote s The Duchess of Malfi (1.1, 87\endash 100). During the play Ferdinand will commit many actions \lquote by a deputy\rquote , including the imprisonment, torture and murder of his own sister. The complex ambiguities and unforeseen consequences of the relationships between lords and those they appoint as their agents lie close to the heart of Webster\rquote s intricate masterpiece. \par
\par
This is territory other leading playwrights of the early seventeenth century also found fascinating. Take, for example, the story of a count, fixated with the beauty of a neighbouring countess, who employs one of his courtiers as an emissary, to woo the lady on his behalf. The countess, however, becomes infatuated with the messenger, not the message. To complicate matters further, the go-between is a woman in male disguise, who is herself in love with the count. Shakespeare\rquote s Twelfth Night demonstrates that a substantial comic harvest can be garnered from observing a nobleman work \lquote by a deputy\rquote  in affairs of the heart. \par
\par
Jacobean revenge tragedy also repeatedly explores the same fertile terrain. In The Revenger\rquote s Tragedy, for instance, the protagonist, Vindice, a man bereft of patrimony or means of advancement, accepts lowly court service from the Duke\rquote s son in order to edge closer to his new employer\rquote s father, whom he plans to kill in revenge for the latter\rquote s murder of Vindice\rquote s own betrothed. The duties assigned to him include attempting to seduce his own sister on behalf of his new employer. As Twelfth Night had done with Viola, so this play relishes mapping the ways in which Vindice is tested to extremes by the unanticipated entanglements intrinsic to his role as courtly go-between. \par
\par
As the Duke is being murdered, during a sexual assignation arranged for him by Vindice, he appeals for help to a servant standing in the shadows, not realising that this man is Vindice\rquote s brother and accomplice. As Hippolito stamps on the Duke\rquote s body, Vindice moralises what we are seeing: \par
\par
Alas, poor lecher, in the hands of knaves, \par
A slavish duke is baser than his slaves. (3.5, 156\endash 7) \par
\par
In this world turned savagely upside down, dukes first degrade themselves, and then meet a fate apt to their self-inflicted ignominy by the agency of go-betweens whose cunning far outbids their own, and into whose hands they have blindly committed their fates. The Revenger\rquote s Tragedy is a text haunted by echoes of Shakespeare\rquote s Hamlet; but the vision it imparts is far removed from that of Shakespeare\rquote s tragedy, where vengeance rests in the hands of the wronged prince himself, rather than any intermediary. \par
\par
Role reversal \par
\par
In Middleton and Rowley\rquote s The Changeling, the aristocratic Beatrice-Joanna commissions her father\rquote s servant, De Flores, whom she loathes, to dispose of the man she is contracted to marry, so that she can replace him with another suitor. She believes that her agent can be paid in money, whereas the reward he has promised himself from her is sexual. Angered by her resistance, De Flores issues an icy command which educates her in the true nature of their relationship: \par
\par
Look but into your conscience, read me there \emdash  \par
\rquote Tis a true book, you\rquote ll find me there your equal. \par
Push! Fly not to your birth, but settle you \par
In what the act has made you\'85 (3.3, 132\endash 5) \par
\par
As in The Revenger\rquote s Tragedy, a servant\rquote s voice judges a person of higher rank with an unassailable authority. The social distance which once defined and circumscribed their relationship has been annihilated by the deeds the servant has done on his mistress\rquote s behalf. \par
\par
In her last moments, Beatrice-Joanna will testify that her fate now lies in \lquote the common sewer\rquote , where all \lquote distinction\rquote  of rank and birth will be obliterated (5.3, 153). Shakespearean tragedy typically selects its protagonists from the elite \emdash  either figures of royal blood or military heroes whose outstanding prowess underpins the stability and survival of the societies they serve. The authors of The Revenger\rquote s Tragedy and The Changeling contribute to what we might call a \lquote tragedy of service\rquote  \emdash  amode of theatre where characters of subordinate social status come to equal, or exceed, in dramatic status the men and women of superior rank whom they serve. The intimacy of access their indispensability to their employers affords them breeds consequences that transform the master/servant relationship. What\rquote s more, these agents upstage their masters to become key protagonists in the plays in which they appear. \par
\par
Contortions \par
\par
The Duchess of Malfi also explores this \lquote tragedy of service\rquote . Its subject is the lethal interplay between three princely siblings \emdash  Ferdinand Duke of Calabria and the Duchess of Malfi, who are twins, and their brother the Cardinal. But their stories are inextricably interwoven with the histories of two men of lower birth but outstanding abilities, Bosola and Antonio. \par
\par
Impoverished and unemployed, Bosola seeks preferment from the brothers. He has already served a sentence in the galleys for a murder \lquote suborned\rquote  (1.1, 67) by the Cardinal, who has privately agreed with Ferdinand that the latter should hire Bosola to spy on their sister, a widow whose remarriage they intend to block. Antonio is the Duchess\rquote s steward, a man of impressive talents, who reveres the Duchess for the rectitude and probity which distinguish her from the serpentine duplicity of her brothers. So, the play contrasts an aspirant for service with a man securely in service; and a needy petitioner willing to do anything for men of ruthless amorality, with a steward who rejoices in the just rule of the lady he serves. \par
\par
In the course of the first act both servants are made offers by their superiors which they hesitate to accept. Bosola is unnerved by Ferdinand\rquote s sudden friendliness, his gift of gold and the offer of employment in the Duchess\rquote s household. The preferment so long coveted, when finally in his grasp, seems like a satanic lure which will consign him to damnation. But, however powerfully he expresses this recoil, his desire to accept the offered post remains urgent. This struggle between conflicting impulses breeds extraordinary contortions of language. Bosola embraces the gift while cursing the giver; bizarrely pleads his fear of seeming ungrateful as an alibi for his being now prepared to \lquote do / All the ill man can invent\rquote ; and mocks the advancement he craves as growing \lquote out of horse dung\rquote , since the appointment dangled before him is to \lquote the provisorship o\rquote th\rquote  horse\rquote  (1.1, 252\endash 78). \par
\par
Picture \par
\par
Patterns \par
\par
If Webster had intended Bosola at this point to seem no more than an assassin, he would have deleted much of the dialogue between Bosola and Ferdinand in this scene. Bosola\rquote s character is, instead, forged from contradictory imperatives which he cannot resist expressing \emdash  a craven need to be employed by those in power, for example, matched with a deep revulsion from the kind of service that employment will entail. Ferdinand tolerates, even provokes, his free-speaking and affects to believe that it is mere masquerade, another indulgent exhibition of his \lquote old garb of melancholy\rquote , and, therefore, the perfect cover for his espionage activities on Ferdinand\rquote s behalf. The pattern in Bosola\rquote s behaviour established here recurs throughout the play. \par
\par
Antonio faces his own crisis of service in Act 1. Believing himself summoned to account for the trust the Duchess has placed in him, he finds himself instead the startled object of her declaration of love and proposal of marriage. In contrast to Bosola\rquote s zigzagging volubility in the scene with Ferdinand, this transformation of his fortunes awes Antonio into dumbfounded silence and then hesitant speech. The magnitude of the gift, the vast disparity of rank, and the danger from her brothers\rquote  inveterate hostility all combine to awe and subdue him. Their marriage, if contracted, could never be made public. As Bosola both desired and was repelled by the promotion he had so avidly pursued, so Antonio, confronted by an offer of marriage he had never anticipated or sought from a woman he so transparently adores, finds himself caught between desire to accept and fear of the consequences such acceptance will bring with it. As he tells her, \lquote he\rquote s a fool / That, being a-cold, would thrust his hands i\rquote th\rquote  fire / To warm them\rquote  (1.1, 416 \endash 18). \par
\par
The delicate social and sexual comedy of this extraordinary scene comes to rest in an exit in which the Duchess gently instructs Antonio in how he should act if he can summon the courage: \lquote lead\rquote  her by the hand and \lquote let\rquote  her \lquote shroud\rquote  her \lquote blushes in\rquote  his \lquote bosom\rquote  (1.1, 485, 492). Contrast the deliberate echo of this moment in The Changeling, where De Flores commands Beatrice-Joanna to \lquote rise, and shroud your blushes in my bosom\rquote  as he forces her to bed (3.3, 166). The tentative complexities of the negotiations in Webster\rquote s scene are substituted with the brutal certainty of a masterful servant as he trains the aristocratic woman he desires to obey his lust. \par
\par
Paradoxes of revenge \par
\par
Neither Bosola nor Antonio is capable of such brutal self-confidence, and Webster devotes much dramatic ingenuity to tracking throughout the play the intricately self-divided nature of their behaviour in their dealings with Ferdinand and the Duchess respectively. An examination of later events highlights the quirky distinctiveness of his invention. \par
\par
When Ferdinand orders Bosola to torment and murder his sister, he does not lay down a detailed scenario as to how this is to be carried out. That is left to Bosola to devise. The envoy or go-between, sent to obey a set of instructions prescribed by his prince, will in practice often find himself confronted with developing circumstances unpredicted in those instructions. He will thus need to improvise in ways which, he must hope, do not clash with his superior\rquote s explicit aims. A space opens up here which, if he so desires, can be exploited by the intermediary. In the nerve-wracking sequence leading up to the Duchess\rquote s final moments, much of what happens is of Bosola\rquote s design, not Ferdinand\rquote s. The cumulative effect of those events is to prompt the Duchess to a self-interrogation which restores to her, as she faces death, a self-possession that the preceding mental torture was intended to prevent. Bosola, intricately self-divided as always, simultaneously obeys and disobeys his master\rquote s commands. \par
\par
The paradoxes proliferate further. In the aftermath of the Duchess\rquote s murder, Ferdinand (who ordered her death) turns on Bosola and rebuffs him. The ultimate consequence of that is Ferdinand\rquote s own death at the hands of the man he had appointed to be his loyal agent and a murderer on his behalf. The Duchess of Malfi is the first English revenge play in which the perpetrator of the central assassination appoints himself to be the revenger of the crime he has himself committed! In this astounding tragedy of service Webster displays an addicted fascination with the selfcontradictions and uncertainties of those who live close to princes and must do their bidding, as well as the dramatic inventiveness to pursue that theme to novel and challenging narrative conclusions. \par
